My DevOps Journey: Day 4 Networking

1. OSI Model (7 Layers)
A blueprint of how networking actually works:
Application (HTTP, FTP, SSH)
Presentation (SSL/TLS)
Session (Managing sessions, e.g., API calls)
Transport (TCP, UDP)
Network (IP addressing, routing)
Data Link (Ethernet, switches)
Physical (Cables, Wi-Fi signals)

2. Common Protocols You Must Know
 HTTP/HTTPS — Web communication
 SSH — Secure server login
 FTP/SFTP — File transfers
 DNS — Resolving domain names
 Load Balancing Protocols — Like TCP/UDP layer load balancers

3. Important Ports to Remember
22 → SSH (Secure Shell)
80 → HTTP (Web traffic)
443 → HTTPS (Secure web traffic)
21 → FTP
25 / 587 → SMTP (Mail)
3306 → MySQL Database
6379 → Redis
27017 → MongoDB

4. Other Networking Concepts
 VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) — Isolated networking in cloud providers.
 NAT (Network Address Translation) — Giving internet access to private servers.
 Load Balancing — Managing traffic efficiently across servers.
 Firewall/Security Groups — Defining what is allowed or blocked.

5. Basic Tools Every DevOps Should Know
 Commands: ping, traceroute, curl, telnet, netstat, tcpdump
 Tools: Wireshark, Netcat, Nmap

6. Subnetting
 Understanding how to break networks into smaller parts improves:
Efficient IP address usage
Better security (by isolating systems)
Easier traffic management in large cloud architectures.
